{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794184377,"nonce":"RnZ6FM3xuBrLjTuI/A7ocOP40jo=","title":"Plan: russl (Rust Shader Language), shaders written in Rust syntax and compiled at build time","message":"Owner decision, 2026-11-09: a shader compiler of our own, named russl (pronounced Russel), in a standalone repository under the project-blinc organisation. Rust and WGSL already share most of their syntax; russl is the macro and compiler that make shader code read as Rust and arrive at run time already compiled.\n\nThis is the \"language of our own\" branch of the lean GPU stack plan (fc977db), and the authoring side of the offline shader pipeline (709d29f). It also answers the SDF duplication (6909945) and is the natural backend for compile-time flow shaders (280f391).\n\n\n1. Shader source in Rust syntax: fn, struct, const, let, if/else, match, for over ranges, loop, method-call builtins (v.dot(w), x.clamp(a, b), v.length()), Rust operators and casts.\n2. Compiled at build time. A proc macro (russl! inline, or an attribute on a module) and a build.rs driver (russl_build, for shader libraries that span files) parse with syn, type-check, lower to a naga Module, validate, and emit artifacts as consts.\n3. One source, every target: WGSL for WebGPU (browsers accept nothing else) and wgpu, plus MSL, HLSL, SPIR-V and GLSL ES through naga's backends, so no shader compiler runs on the user's machine.\n4. Host types from the shader: every shader struct, binding and override gets a generated Rust twin with the WGSL layout (offsets and padding from naga's layouter), bytemuck Pod and a const size assert. Replaces the hand-kept mirrors that tests/primitive_layout.rs guards today.\n5. Errors at the Rust token that caused them, through syn spans and a span table into naga's validator. Never a line number in generated WGSL.\n6. Variants without copies: one body specialised per tier (storage buffer, data texture, vertex buffer) by compile-time accessor selection, the load_aux idea in 6909945, so sdf_*, *_dt and *_vb become one source each.\n\n\n- Not rust-gpu: no rustc codegen backend, no arbitrary Rust, no std, no heap, no closures, no traits in the first version.\n- No runtime compilation by default (an opt-in WGSL-only path stays possible for hot reload).\n- No features beyond what WGSL and naga can express: no buffer pointers or bindless heaps. That is the case for Slang in 709d29f; if a root-arguments design needs them, russl cannot provide them for WebGPU anyway.\n\n\n    russl! {\n        struct VsOut { #[builtin(position)] pos: Vec4, #[location(0)] uv: Vec2 }\n\n        #[group(0)] #[binding(0)] static PRIMS: Storage\u003c[Primitive]\u003e;\n        #[override] const FEAT_SHADOW: bool = true;\n\n        #[vertex]\n        fn vs(#[builtin(vertex_index)] vi: u32) -\u003e VsOut { ... }\n\n        #[fragment]\n        fn fs(input: VsOut) -\u003e #[location(0)] Vec4 {\n            let d = sd_rounded_rect(input.uv, size, radius);\n            Vec4::new(1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 - d.clamp(0.0, 1.0))\n        }\n    }\n\nExpands to a module with WGSL (and the other targets when enabled) as consts, the entry point names, a generated Overrides struct (FEAT_SHADOW today is an override constant set by name and f64), the bind group layout description, and the Rust twins of the shader structs.\n\nDecisions to make in M1: swizzles as fields (v.xy) or methods (v.xy()); vector constructors (Vec4::new or vec4); unsuffixed float literals as f32 (WGSL abstract float) rather than Rust's f64; glam-style type names or WGSL names.\n\n\nproject-blinc/russl, Apache-2.0 like Blinc.\n\n- russl: facade, re-exports the macro and the small runtime types (artifact consts, layout traits). No naga at run time.\n- russl_compiler: pure library. syn AST, typed IR, lowering to naga::Module, validation, backends. Shared by the macro, the build driver and the CLI.\n- russl_macros: thin proc macro over the compiler.\n- russl_build: build.rs driver for multi-file libraries with use between modules. A proc macro sees only tokens and cannot read other items or crates; a driver that reads the files sidesteps that.\n- russl_cli: russl check and russl emit --target wgsl|msl|hlsl|spv|glsl, for CI and for reading what a macro produced.\n\nnaga is pinned to the version Blinc's wgpu uses (=26.0.0 today) and bumped together.\n\n\nM0. Repository, crates, CI on Linux, macOS and Windows (fmt, clippy, tests, naga validation of goldens), licence, README.\nM1. Core language: scalars, vectors, matrices, arrays, structs, functions, control flow, entry points, bindings, overrides, lowered to naga and emitted as WGSL. Gate: golden WGSL tests that validate, trybuild compile-fail tests that check the error span.\nM2. Blinc pilot, two consumers. sdf_simple.wgsl (small, self-contained, with a pixel parity test in blinc_recorder plain_shader_parity.rs). Gate: same pixels, same binding layout. Then flow!: lower blinc_flow's IR to russl instead of WGSL text. Gate: the F2 tests (generated WGSL validates; demos unchanged).\nM3. Host interop: generated Rust twins, Overrides, bind group layout descriptors. Gate: the ported shaders' entries in primitive_layout.rs become a generated assert.\nM4. Modules and variants: use between russl modules (russl_build), monomorphised generics (const and scalar type parameters), tier accessors. Port sdf_core with its _dt and _vb copies as one source. Gate: the existing SDF pixel suites and the DT packing guards in 6909945's follow-up.\nM5. Offline backends: MSL, HLSL, SPIR-V and GLSL ES at build time; wgpu passthrough shader modules where wgpu 26 supports them (to verify); naga leaves Blinc's runtime for ported shaders. Gate: the HLSL DXC CI job, iPhone and Android device runs, the WebGL2 check, clean build time measured against today.\nM6. Port the remaining shaders and delete the WGSL sources.\n\n\n- Readable output: names kept, WGSL dumpable (CLI, env var), or debugging moves from WGSL to guessing.\n- Build cost: naga runs in the proc macro for every crate that uses russl. naga is already compiled for wgpu; measure the macro's share in M2.\n- Cross-crate shared types: a struct used by shaders in several crates needs either russl_build or a types library re-exported by both. Decide in M3.\n- flow: its statement language stays as a graph front end lowering to russl, or flow shaders become plain russl fragment functions. Lowering keeps today's users working; decide in M2.\n- Prior art to read before M1: WESL (WGSL extended with imports and generics, wesl-rs), naga_oil (imports, used by Bevy), rust-gpu, Slang. russl's niche is Rust syntax inside the Rust toolchain, generated host types, and no external tool.\n- Scope beyond Blinc: a standalone repository suggests russl serves other projects too; Blinc-only pieces (flow, the SDF tiers) stay in Blinc.\n\nRelated: fc977db (lean GPU stack plan), 709d29f (offline shader pipeline), 6909945 (SDF shader copies), 280f391 (flow shaders at compile time).","files":null}]}